.TH TUI_SCHEME_LOAD 3 2026-10-22 "libtuikit 0.13" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_scheme_load, tui_scheme_parse, tui_scheme_defaults, tui_scheme_builtin,
tui_scheme_init, tui_scheme_style, tui_scheme_diagnostic, tui_role_name,
tui_role_from_name, tui_color_reduce \-
colour schemes: the style of every role from a scheme file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/scheme.h>
.PP
.BI "int  tui_scheme_load(tui_scheme *" sc ", const char *" name ", tui_scheme_report *" rep );
.BI "int  tui_scheme_parse(tui_scheme *" sc ", const char *" text ", const char *" origin ", tui_scheme_report *" rep );
.BI "void tui_scheme_defaults(tui_scheme *" sc );
.BI "tui_style tui_scheme_builtin(tui_role " role );
.B "int  tui_scheme_init(void);"
.BI "tui_style tui_scheme_style(tui_role " role );
.B "const char *tui_scheme_diagnostic(void);"
.BI "const char *tui_role_name(tui_role " role );
.BI "int  tui_role_from_name(const char *" name );
.BI "tui_color tui_color_reduce(tui_color " c ", int " colors );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The roles of the interface (the workspace's TUI model) each have a style.
They are read from a scheme file so that the user can change them; a program
asks the library for a role and chooses no colour of its own.  The palette
macros of
.I tuikit/screen.h
keep their values.
.SS Roles
.B TUI_ROLE_TEXT ,
.B HEADER ,
.B STATUS ,
.B SELECTION ,
.B CURSOR ,
.B ACCENT ,
.B FRAME ,
.B INPUT ,
.B INPUT_FOCUS ,
.B TAB_ACTIVE ,
.B TAB_INACTIVE ,
.B OK ,
.B ERROR ,
.B HINT
and
.BR PROGRESS .
The keys in a file are
.BR text ,
.BR header ,
.BR status ,
.BR selection ,
.BR cursor ,
.BR accent ,
.BR frame ,
.BR input ,
.BR input.focus ,
.BR tab.active ,
.BR tab.inactive ,
.BR ok ,
.BR error ,
.B hint
and
.BR progress .
.BR tui_role_name ()
gives the key of a role (NULL for a value that is none) and
.BR tui_role_from_name ()
the role of a key (\-1 for none).
.SS File format
A scheme is a plain text file
.IB NAME .scheme
with one
.RI "``" "key = fg bg attr,attr" ''
per line.
A line whose first non-blank character is
.B #
is a comment, and so is everything from a
.B #
in the third place on; in the first two places a
.B #
starts a
.B #rrggbb
colour.
A part may be
.B \-
for the terminal's own, and trailing parts may be left out.
.PP
Colours:
.BR default ,
.BR black ,
.BR red ,
.BR green ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR blue ,
.BR magenta ,
.BR cyan ,
.BR white ,
each also with
.B bright\-
before it, an index from 0 to 255, or
.BR #rrggbb .
Attributes:
.BR bold ,
.BR dim ,
.BR italic ,
.BR underline ,
.BR strike ,
.B reverse
and
.BR blink .
Lines are at most 255 bytes and a file at most 65536 bytes.
.PP
.nf
# example
error      = red - bold
selection  = - #000080 -
header     = white blue bold
tab.active = cyan - bold,underline
.fi
.SS Search order
.IB NAME .scheme
is looked up in
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tuikit/schemes/
(or
.I ~/.config/tuikit/schemes/
when that is unset or not an absolute path) first, then in
.IR /usr/share/libtuikit/schemes/ ;
a user's scheme of a name wins over the system's.
A name is letters, digits,
.BR _ ,
.B \-
and
.BR . ,
at most 63 characters, not starting with a dot.
.PP
The name in use is set by the line
.B "scheme = NAME"
in
.I ~/.config/tuikit/tuikit.conf
(the user directory above), then in
.IR /etc/tuikit/tuikit.conf ;
when neither names one, the built-in palette is used.
The package installs
.I /usr/share/libtuikit/schemes/default.scheme
with exactly the built-in styles.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.BR tui_scheme_load ()
fills
.I sc
with the built-in styles and applies the scheme
.I name
over them; with
.I name
NULL the name comes from
.IR tuikit.conf .
The scheme named
.B default
is the built-in palette when its file is not installed.
It returns the number of problems; the scheme is usable whatever it is.
.PP
.BR tui_scheme_parse ()
applies the lines of
.I text
to
.IR sc ;
.I origin
names the text in the report.
It returns the number of problems found in it.
.PP
.BR tui_scheme_defaults ()
sets every role to
.BR tui_scheme_builtin ().
.PP
.BR tui_scheme_init ()
loads the active scheme, the one
.I tuikit.conf
selects, and returns the number of problems; the first query does it by
itself, and calling it again reloads.
.BR tui_scheme_style ()
is the style of a role in it (the terminal's default for a bad role) and
.BR tui_scheme_diagnostic ()
the first problem of that load, or NULL: the program shows it once.
The active scheme is process-wide; initialise it before starting threads.
.PP
.BR tui_color_reduce ()
maps a colour to what a terminal of
.I colors
(256, 16 or 8) shows: at 256 the nearest of the colour cube and the greys,
below that the channels from 64 up decide the hue (a dark blue stays blue)
and, at 16, a channel from 192 up makes it bright.
The default colour is unchanged.
.SS The report
.nf
typedef struct tui_scheme_report {
    int  problems;
    char first[192];   /* "origin:line: what" */
} tui_scheme_report;
.fi
.SH ERRORS
None is an error in the sense of the return value: a missing, unreadable, too
long or malformed file, line, key or value is counted as a problem, and the
role keeps its built-in style.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libtuikit (3),
.BR tui_screen_new (3)
